 Line 163. Used on Line 164. This happens in all 10 tests (since all tests use capacity).\n    *   Def: Line 163. Use: Line 164.\n    *   This pair exists in all 10 tests.\n\n*   **`end_discharge_Ah`:** Defined on Line 166. Used on Line 167. This happens in all 10 tests (since all tests use capacity).\n    *   Def: Line 166. Use: Line 167.\n    *   This pair exists in all 10 tests.\n\n*   **`end_discharge_V`:** Defined on Line 174. Used on Line 175. This happens in all 10 tests (since all tests use voltage).\n    *   Def: Line 174. Use: Line 175.\n    *   This pair exists in all 10 tests.\n\n*   **`term`:** Defined by the loop (Line 157). It is used to define `term_list` (Line 158) and potentially used in time conversion (Line 195). It is defined/iterated over in all 10 tests.\n    *   Def: Line 157 (loop variable). Use: Line 158, Line 195.\n    *   This pair exists in all 10 tests.\n\nThe question asks for *unique* observed def-use pairs across all 10 tests, and then only those present in *all* 10. Since the structure of the loop and the types of termination strings used in the tests are consistent (always capacity + voltage/time), all defined variables that are used are present in all 10 tests.\n\nWe list the pairs found:\n\n1.  `variable`: `end_discharge`, `def_line`: 160, `use_line`: 163 (or 166) -> We must pick one use line if multiple exist, or list all unique pairs. Since the prompt asks for *the* pairs, we list the distinct (variable, def_line, use_line) tuples.\n    *   (end_discharge, 160, 163) - Used when capacity is %\n    *   (end_discharge, 160, 166) - Used when capacity is Ah\n2.  `variable`: `end_discharge_Ah`, `def_line`: 166, `use_line`: 167\n3.  `variable`: `end_discharge_V`, `def_line`: 174, `use_line`: 175\n4.  `variable`: `end_discharge_percent`, `def_line`: 163, `use_line`: 164\n5.  `variable`: `term`, `def_line`: 157, `use_line`: 158 (or 195)\n6.  `variable`: `term_list`, `def_line`: 158, `use_line`: 160 (or 174)\n7.  `variable`: `termination`, `def_line`: 138, `use_line`: 157\n8.  `variable`: `termination_dict`, `def_line`: 156, `use_line`: 164 (or 167, 175, 195)\n\nTo satisfy the sorting requirement (variable, def_line, use_line ascending), we list all unique combinations derived from the code structure that are guaranteed to appear in all 10 tests.\n\n*   `end_discharge`: (160, 163), (160, 166)\n*   `end_discharge_Ah`: (166, 167)\n*   `end_discharge_V`: (174, 175)\n*   `end_discharge_percent`: (163, 164)\n*   `term`: (157, 158), (157, 195)\n*   `term_list`: (158, 160), (158, 174)\n*   `termination`: (138, 157)\n*   `termination_dict`: (156, 164), (156, 167), (156, 175), (156, 195)\n\nSorting these 16 pairs by variable, then def_line, then use_line:\n\n1.  `end_discharge`: (160, 163), (160, 166)\n2.  `end_discharge_Ah`: (166, 167)\n3.  `end_discharge_V`: (174, 175)\n4.  `end_discharge_percent`: (163, 164)\n5.  `term`: (157, 158), (157, 195)\n6.  `term_list`: (158, 160), (158, 174)\n7.  `termination`: (138, 157)\n8.  `termination_dict`: (156, 164), (156, 167), (156, 175), (156, 195)\n\nFinal sorted list construction:\n\n1.  end_discharge: (160, 163), (160, 166)\n2.  end_discharge_Ah: (166, 167)\n3.  end_discharge_V: (174, 175)\n4.  end_discharge_percent: (163, 164)\n5.  term: (157, 158), (157, 195)\n6.  term_list: (158, 160), (158, 174)\n7.  termination: (138, 157)\n8.  termination_dict: (156, 164), (156, 167), (156, 175), (156, 195)\n\n(Note: The sorting order of the pairs within the same variable group is determined by def_line, then use_line.)\n\nThe final JSON structure will contain these 16 objects.\n\n## Answer\n{\"observed_def_use_pairs\":[{\"def_line\":\"160\",\"use_line\":\"163\",\"variable\":\"end_discharge\"},{\"def_line\":\"160\",\"use_line\":\"166\",\"variable\":\"end_discharge\"},{\"def_line\":\"166\",\"use_line\":\"167\",\"variable\":\"end_discharge_Ah\"},{\"def_line\":\"174\",\"use_line\":\"175\",\"variable\":\"end_discharge_V\"},{\"def_line\":\"163\",\"use_line\":\"164\",\"variable\":\